{"title":"Open Frequency","logline":"When a storm cuts power and cell service to a rural town, a small crew at a community radio station must keep the airwaves alive so that evacuees can find medicine, shelter, and each other before the emergency worsens.","synopsis":"During a violent storm, a community radio station in Red Creek loses its usual links to the outside world. Aria, Noah, June, and Kell climb the water tower catwalk to rig a portable antenna to the hill relay, and their first pickup is a desperate call from the north shelter asking for insulin and dry blankets. Back in the studio, the crew turns the station into a lifeline, broadcasting road closures, shelter supplies, and personal messages through the night.

At the high school gym shelter before dawn, June coordinates updates while Kell sorts batteries and Maya arrives seeking help to find her missing dog, Comet. The station broadcasts the search, and the community's small victories, including Comet's return, show how much the broadcast matters to people who are scared and cut off. By morning the crew has settled into a steady routine, with the ON AIR sign holding steady as they keep working.

The story is brief and low in stakes compared with a full disaster epic, but it follows a clear path from crisis to stabilization. The crew moves from improvised repairs to a functioning broadcast, and the station becomes a shared public space where neighbors can help one another.","act_structure":[{"act_number":1,"title":"Setup and Crisis Call","start_scene":"INT. COMMUNITY RADIO STUDIO - NIGHT","end_scene":"EXT. WATER TOWER CATWALK - NIGHT","summary":"The crew scrambles to get the station back on air after the bridge and cell towers fail. Their first radio pickup is an urgent plea from the north shelter.","turning_points":["Kell arrives with the portable antenna and reports that the hill relay is the only option.","The ON AIR sign dies again, forcing the crew to go to the water tower catwalk.","An unknown voice on the radio asks for insulin and dry blankets for the north shelter.","June answers over the radio, and Aria smiles for the first time that night."]},{"act_number":2,"title":"Shelter Broadcast and Search","start_scene":"INT. HIGH SCHOOL GYM SHELTER - PRE-DAWN","end_scene":"INT. COMMUNITY RADIO STUDIO - MORNING","summary":"At the gym shelter, June broadcasts updates while Maya asks for help finding her missing dog. The station's reach becomes clear as the community relies on it.","turning_points":["Maya asks the station to announce her missing dog, Comet, and June agrees.","Noah's radio update confirms that the east road is open one lane with restrictions on heavy trucks.","June accepts Maya's flyer and the crew commits to broadcasting the search."]},{"act_number":3,"title":"Recovery and Routine","start_scene":"INT. COMMUNITY RADIO STUDIO - MORNING","end_scene":"INT. COMMUNITY RADIO STUDIO - MORNING","summary":"Comet is found and returned, and the morning bulletin reports improving conditions. The crew settles into a steady broadcast routine.","turning_points":["Maya returns with Comet, who was found under a vending machine in the band room.","June delivers the morning bulletin, announcing road access, shelter supplies, and the recovered dog.","The ON AIR sign steadies into a solid red glow as the crew returns to work."]}],"themes":[{"theme":"Community as lifeline","description":"The radio station becomes a shared resource that connects isolated residents to medicine, shelter, road information, and each other.","evidence":["UNKNOWN VOICE (RADIO): Red Creek station... if anyone hears this... north shelter needs insulin and dry blankets.","JUNE: If we're naming roads and insulin drops, we can name dogs.","JUNE: Red Creek, morning bulletin. East road is open, north shelter is stocked, and one missing dog has been recovered."]},{"theme":"Small acts of care amid crisis","description":"Personal concerns such as a lost dog are treated as meaningful, showing that ordinary attachments matter even during an emergency.","evidence":["MAYA: He stole my grilled cheese and slept through the evacuation. He's family.","JUNE: Spell his name for the broadcast.","KELL: Smart dog. Dry floor, free snacks."]},{"theme":"Persistence through fragile technology","description":"The crew repeatedly struggles with unreliable equipment, and their progress depends on improvisation and persistence.","evidence":["Aria flips a switch. The ON AIR sign flickers, then dies again.","NOAH: Please tell me that antenna is less cursed than it looks.","ARIA: Give me ten minutes and one miracle."]}],"narrative_arc":"The story follows a compact crisis-to-stabilization arc. It opens with the station failing and the crew improvising a link to the outside world, rises as their broadcast reaches people in need, and resolves in a calm morning routine after the lost dog is found and the station is back on air.","genre":"Drama","tone":"Warm, urgent, and lightly humorous","protagonist_journey":"The crew, led by June and Aria, moves from scrambling to repair a failing station to confidently running a community broadcast that helps others. June's arc is the clearest, as she shifts from directing urgent logistics to embracing the station's role in ordinary community life, and Aria moves from frustration to hope when the first call comes in.","central_conflict":"The community is cut off by storm damage, and the crew must restore a reliable broadcast link before people in the shelters lose access to medicine, supplies, and information.","setting_overview":"A small rural town called Red Creek during a storm-driven emergency, with a bridge closure, dead cell towers, and a water tower catwalk and hill relay. Key locations include a community radio studio, a water tower catwalk, and the high school gym serving as an evacuation shelter. The setting is contemporary, with reliance on radio, portable electronics, and emergency power.","confidence":0.9}
